In this lesson, we'll look at linear functions, the simplest kind of graph you'll meet. A linear function has a rule made up of an x term and a plain number, and its graph is always a straight line. The number in front of the x is called the gradient, which tells you how steep the line is, basically how much the y value changes each time x goes up by one. To find the gradient between any two points, you take the difference in y values and divide by the difference in x values. The plain number on its own is called the y-intercept, and that's simply the point where the line crosses the up-and-down axis. If you want the x-intercept, where the line crosses sideways, you set the whole rule equal to zero and solve for x. Now here's the handy part. Two lines are parallel when they have exactly the same gradient. And two lines are perpendicular when their gradients multiplied together give negative one, so each gradient is the negative reciprocal of the other. Keep that in your back pocket, it comes up a lot. Now let's see it in action with a worked example.